    "turn_text": "The thing that I say, unless they let us sit down and negotiate what they have left out and tell me why you left them out or why you thought other things were more important than basically aid to our children and youth homelessness. I mean, my goodness, we have over 10,000 growing rapidly. We're throwing families into disarray. You have a COVID-19 pandemic on top of an opiate epidemic. And then just with that, the dysfunctions of families today. And we're leaving kids behind. And I said, you know, we have a bill out by self. They sent for Kalski Republican from Alaska. It's bipartisan all the way. Every state has this challenge in the problem with homelessness for our children. And so we put a bill out, which is called the Emergency Family Stabilization Act. It's an 800 million. 800 million is an awful lot of money. But in the scheme of a trillion dollars, that's not even one percent, one tenth of one percent. It's just unbelievable and we said it would do so much good. We have another, there's nothing in there for them and basically you have all of these emergency service, administration for children and families are just overwhelmed and they're telling you they can't keep up with it, the man they have. There's no dedication for students for rural hospitals. We've lost three hospitals in West Virginia. We're defined as a total rural state, which we are. And with that, There's been $175 billion that was supposed to go to hospitals. The first tranche of money, $113 billion went out the door. Only $11 billion went to rural hospitals. Now they're putting another $25 billion for hospitals. No dedication for rural. So we're fighting for the formula I put in was a 20%. The reason I chose 20% was that's what rural hospitals serve in America. They serve 20% of the American population. That means $29 billion more has to go to rural hospitals for us to save them. If not, we're in jeopardy. That's not in the bill. There's no funding at all day for hotspots or broadband and internet connectivity. Now, here we are. Stay-at-home orders. People shut in. We have schools not sure. They have their schools are giving options whether you have just don't learn. That means stay at home. If you don't have connectivity, you don't have iPads. How",
