    "turn_idx": 55,
    "turn_text": "Well, sir, I don't have any of my Republican friends and colleagues that are opposing because most of them have signed on as a co-sponsor with me. So that's basically the first indication that you should be able to move something if you have both sides agreeable or both senators on a Democrat and Republican senators, which are both agreeing this is something that needs to be fixed. So hopefully with that, a lot of this is in the bill that came from the House. The House bill was $3 trillion, which we're not going to go in that direction. I can tell you it will not get to that. But there'll be an adjustment from the trillion dollars to find out how do we keep people basically up and right. Now, how do we keep people that basically no father of their own said, I lost their job? Because of the health crisis we had, we showed them down, shut the business down, and people lost their job. We should have kept those people whole. They should have been able to continue to receive their paycheck, because it wasn't their fault until the economy started opening up and coming back. Well, now there's a lot of confrontation over the $600. It's too much, and basically people basically are making more than they did when they worked, and it goes on and on and on. I've always been for keeping them whole, 100% of their paycheck. But with that being said, now we're down from the sixth time they said we will offer $200. So we have to find a balance there. And I think everyone's agreed there'll be another stimulus package going out, whether it's a $1,200 or whatever, but there's another one coming out. I think there's agreement there. So the things that we can agree is what we should pass next week. And this thing that we have agreement on these uh... hot items which are not high cost at all should be something that really help a lot of less virginians absolutely so i'm going to be pushing and swinging as much as i can",
